Three accents on the Foundation's mono white-on-black base. The base is
set by `Manifolding-Emacs-Foundation' before the splash, so this file only
adds the accents and deliberately does not restate the base — restating it
would win the race instead of cooperating with it.

No icons anywhere: the bar signals with colour and letters, and colour is
the only thing here that can go wrong on a monochrome terminal. Each accent
therefore also differs in the *text* it accompanies, so a terminal without
colour still reads correctly.

** The accents
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defface manifolding-modeline-modified
  '((t :foreground "#FFAF00"))
  "Accent for the modified flag.
Amber. Unique to `modified' — nothing else in the bar is amber, so an
unsaved buffer is the one thing that can be read at a glance."
  :group 'manifolding-modeline)

(defface manifolding-modeline-alert
  '((t :foreground "#FF5F5F" :weight bold))
  "Accent for load errors.
Red and bold. The only bold segment in the bar, because it is the only
one that means something is wrong."
  :group 'manifolding-modeline)

(defface manifolding-modeline-ok
  '((t :foreground "#5FAF5F"))
  "Accent for a healthy reading: a clean branch, an up-to-date index.
Green. Deliberately dim — the common case should not shout."
  :group 'manifolding-modeline)

(defface manifolding-modeline-info
  '((t :foreground "#5FAFD7"))
  "Accent for neutral status: atlas and database state.
Blue. The cool counterpart to the green, and the only face used on the
atlas row."
  :group 'manifolding-modeline)
#+end_src

** A dimmer for inactive windows
`mode-line-inactive' already distinguishes an unfocused window, so the
accents inherit that rather than competing with it. Nothing to set — the
inactive face is the Foundation's, and these accents blend with it.

** Manual extract — Properties in the Mode Line
Migrated from `the-screen/display/06-screen'. This is the reference for
`manifolding-modeline-accent', which is the only place the bar applies
styling.

Certain text properties are meaningful in the mode line. The face property
affects the appearance of text; the help-echo property associates help
strings with the text, and keymap can make the text mouse-sensitive.

There are four ways to specify text properties for text in the mode line:

Put a string with a text property directly into the mode line data
structure, but see The Data Structure of the Mode Line for caveats for
that.

Put a text property on a mode line %-construct such as '%12b'; then the
expansion of the %-construct will have that same text property.

Use a (:propertize elt props…) construct to give elt a text property
specified by props.

Use a list containing :eval form in the mode line data structure, and make
form evaluate to a string that has a text property.

You can use the keymap property to specify a keymap. This keymap only takes
real effect for mouse clicks; binding character keys and function keys to it
has no effect, since it is impossible to move point into the mode line.

When the mode line refers to a variable which does not have a non-nil
risky-local-variable property, any text properties given or specified
within that variable's values are ignored. This is because such properties
could otherwise specify functions to be called, and those functions could
come from file local variables.

Note the third form above — the `propertize' applied to an already rendered
string, which is exactly what the right-align helper does. It is legal on
any version, unlike `mode-line-format-right-align'.
